</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>nDCG@10 results for different view token designs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the impact of view token design on re-ranking performance, we compare three alternative configurations:
(1) First 4 Tokens: Following the FiD-Light <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hofstätter et al. (2023)</a></cite> approach, the first four tokens in the input prompt are reused without introducing dedicated special tokens;
(2) Numeric Tokens: View tokens are replaced with number-based tokens (1, 2, 3, 4);
(3) Alphabetic Tokens: Character-based tokens (A, B, C, D) are used as view tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.2.1 Designs for View Tokens ‣ C.2 Robustness to Selection Bias ‣ Appendix C Analysis of External Biases ‣ Multi-view-guided Passage Reranking with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> demonstrates that the <span id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;extra_id&gt;</span> tokens defined in the T5 tokenizer, as adopted by the proposed MVP framework, are the most effective. This result suggests that: (1) Learnable token embeddings specifically trained to encode query-passage relevance are more effective than simply reusing initial prompt tokens. (2) Moreover, numeric and alphabetic identifiers may already carry semantic meaning from pretraining, leading to potential conflicts with their intended function as compression tokens, ultimately resulting in degraded performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">C.2.2 </span>Identifier Reordering</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing generation-based listwise rerankers rely on identifier tokens to produce outputs, which can potentially introduce selection bias. To further test whether the bias exists in other models, we conducted experiments where the initial passage order was fixed, but only the identifiers were permuted. Two settings were considered: (1) <span id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shuf.</span>, where identifiers were randomly shuffled across three runs, with results averaged; and (2) <span id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rev.</span>, where identifiers were simply reversed (e.g.,"[id<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub">20</sub>]: context<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub">1</sub>, [id<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sub">19</sub>]: context<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sub">2</sub>, …, [id<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_sub">1</sub>]: context<sub id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_sub">20</sub>").</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, summarized in Table <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ C.2.2 Identifier Reordering ‣ C.2 Robustness to Selection Bias ‣ Appendix C Analysis of External Biases ‣ Multi-view-guided Passage Reranking with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, show that models using numeric or alphabetic identifiers suffer performance drops even under such minor reordering, indicating that selection bias is a significant issue. Even in ListT5 with the FiD architecture, we observe minor performance variations when identifier orders are altered, which we attribute to changes in the identifier tokens assigned to passages. However, we address this bias by introducing randomly initialized view tokens. MVP directly computes relevance scores from passage-specific vectors encoded for each query–passage pair, allowing effective listwise reranking even when the same view token is used across passages.</p>
</div>
